Found while measuring the CryptX boundaries for ADR 0004 (karr #43). Not fixed there; recorded rather than folded into an ADR-only change.

Two copies:
  lib/File/SOPS/Encrypted.pm  sub _random_bytes   (the 32-byte IV per value)
  lib/File/SOPS.pm            sub _random_bytes   (the 32-byte data key)

They are byte-identical apart from where one line wraps. This is the twin
shape the distribution keeps getting caught by, applied to the CSPRNG rather
than to the value conversion: the two cannot currently disagree about anything
observable, but nothing stops them, and one of them generates the data key.

Two things worth deciding at the same time, both in the shared body:

1. The fallback is silent. If Crypt::PRNG fails to load, both fall back to
   reading /dev/urandom, and neither says so. A caller who thinks they are
   getting a CSPRNG-backed key gets whatever the fallback produced, with no
   signal either way. "Fail loud" would argue for croaking instead: CryptX is
   already a hard runtime prerequisite (Crypt::AuthEnc::GCM), so Crypt::PRNG
   being absent means the install is broken, not that a fallback is wanted.

2. The fallback does not check its read:

       read $fh, $bytes, $length;
       close $fh;
       return $bytes;

   read() can return short, and returns undef on error. A short return here
   yields a key or an IV of the wrong length with no error. For the IV that is
   a wrong-length GCM nonce -- ADR-relevant, since 32 bytes is part of the wire
   format, not a preference. Should be a checked read (or read_random /
   sysread loop) with a croak.

Suggested shape: one helper, in File::SOPS::Encrypted (which is where the wire
format lives and which File::SOPS already loads), called by both; no silent
fallback; checked read if a fallback is kept at all.

No test covers the fallback path today, so a fix wants one that forces it.
Priority raised to high by the orchestrating agent. The duplication is the cosmetic half; the unchecked read is not. A short read from /dev/urandom yields a data key or a GCM nonce of the wrong LENGTH with no error raised -- that is a cryptographic defect in the fallback path, not a refactor. The silent fallback compounds it: a missing Crypt::PRNG downgrades the CSPRNG without anyone being told.

Sequenced for tonight after karr #35.
Fixed and pinned, not committed (tree left dirty as instructed).

One helper: File::SOPS::Encrypted::_random_bytes, called by encrypt_value for
the nonce and directly by File::SOPS::encrypt for the data key. No cycle --
File::SOPS already loads Encrypted, not the reverse. The copy in File::SOPS.pm
is gone.

The unchecked read, measured before fixing (CryptX 0.087, sops 3.13.3, real
binary at ~/bin/sops):

  data key -> 16 bytes   our encrypt OK, our decrypt OK, `sops -d` exit 0
  data key -> 24 bytes   our encrypt OK, our decrypt OK, `sops -d` exit 0
  data key -> 20/31      dies inside CryptX as "Invalid key size", blamed on gcm
  iv -> 12 bytes         written as 12, our decrypt OK, `sops -d` exit 0
  iv ->  1 byte          written as  1, our decrypt OK, `sops -d` exit 0
  iv ->  0 bytes         dies inside CryptX as CRYPT_ERROR

So a short read ran all the way onto the wire and past the reference binary.
16 and 24 are valid AES key sizes; GCM does not fix the nonce length, so a
1-byte nonce is a working nonce out of a 256-value space. Nothing between the
CSPRNG and sops looks at either length, which is why the check has to be at the
source. It croaks naming only lengths, never bytes.

The fallback is deleted, not repaired. Crypt::PRNG ships in CryptX, the same
installed distribution as the Crypt::AuthEnc::GCM that Encrypted.pm uses at
compile time (verified: same .packlist), so the eval could not fail in any
install able to compile the file -- unreachable code that would have silently
downgraded the CSPRNG if reached. Crypt::PRNG is now a compile-time use.

IV size checked against the reference, not changed: sops 3.13.3 writes iv=32
bytes and tag=16 for every value. The 32-byte nonce in Encrypted.pm is correct.

Regression test t/21-random-bytes.t replaces Crypt::PRNG::random_bytes with a
truncating source and requires the wrong length to be refused before it becomes
a key or a nonce; it also asserts there is no second _random_bytes, and that the
error does not carry the bytes. Against HEAD 4f17de5 (clean worktree, test
copied in): 6 of 7 subtests fail, 32 of 42 assertions. The 7th is the anchor
subtest (32-byte iv, 16-byte tag, round trip) and passes on both.

Full suite with interop: SOPS_BIN=$HOME/bin/sops prove -lr t/ -> 22 files, 506
tests, all pass. t/04-interop.t RAN (sops 3.13.3 at ~/bin/sops, no skip_all,
no skipped assertions anywhere in the suite).

No ADR: no wire bytes move. A correctly generated document is byte-identical
before and after; only a broken CSPRNG behaves differently. The measurements
above live in the comment on _random_bytes and in the test header, which is
where a future reader deciding to delete the length check would look.

